                     /note="WP_005804634.1 hypothetical protein (Acinetobacter)
                     [pid:90.5%, q_cov:100.0%, s_cov:81.4%, Eval:6.2e-92]"
                     /note="Domain:cl00208:RNase_T2 N/A. Ribonuclease T2 (RNase
                     T2) is a widespread family of secreted RNases found in
                     every organism examined thus far.  This family includes
                     RNase Rh, RNase MC1, RNase LE, and self-incompatibility
                     RNases (S-RNases).  Plant T2 RNases are expressed during
                     leaf senescence in order to scavenge phosphate from
                     ribonucleotides. They are also expressed in response to
                     wounding or pathogen invasion. S-RNases are thought to
                     prevent self-fertilization by acting as selective
                     cytotoxins of 'self' pollen. Generally, RNases have two
                     distinct binding sites: the primary site (B1 site) and the
                     subsite (B2 site), for nucleotides located at the 5'- and
                     3'- terminal ends of the sessil bond, respectively. This CD
                     includes the prokaryotic RNase T2 family members.
                     [Superfamily, Aligned:7-175, Eval:1.6e-13, score:63.6,
                     N-term missing]"
                     /note="MGA_546"
